# Solution — GIAM 5.3 Problem 10

## Problem

Give an inductive proof that $ \forall n\ge3 \in\mathbb{N} $, $ 3n^{2}+3n+1 < 2n^{3} $.

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;D(k+1) = D(k) + 6k^{2}-4, \quad D(n) = 2n^{3}-\left(3n^{2}+3n+1\right)\;}
$$$

The gap $ D $ starts positive at the basis, $ D(3) = 54-37 = 17 $, and only grows, since $ 6k^{2}-4 > 0 $ for $ k\ge1 $. Verified for every $ n $ from $ 3 $ to $ 5000 $; the inequality fails at $ n = 0,1,2 $.

## The Proof

Write $ L(n) = 3n^{2}+3n+1 $ and $ R(n) = 2n^{3} $.

> **Theorem.** For every natural number $ n\ge3 $, $ L(n) < R(n) $.
>
> **Proof.** By induction on $ n $, starting at $ 3 $.
>
> **Basis.** At $ n=3 $, $ L(3) = 27+9+1 = 37 $ and $ R(3) = 54 $, and $ 37 < 54 $.
>
> **Inductive step.** Let $ k\ge3 $ be a particular but arbitrarily chosen natural number and suppose $ L(k) < R(k) $. Expanding both sides at $ k+1 $,
>
> $ \begin{aligned} L(k+1) &= 3(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1)+1 = L(k) + 6k+6 , \\ R(k+1) &= 2(k+1)^{3} = R(k) + 6k^{2}+6k+2 . \end{aligned} $
>
> The right side gains more than the left, because
>
> $ \left(6k^{2}+6k+2\right)-\left(6k+6\right) = 6k^{2}-4 > 0 \quad\text{for } k\ge1 . $
>
> Combining this with the hypothesis,
>
> $ \begin{aligned} L(k+1) &= L(k)+6k+6 \\ &< R(k)+6k+6 \\ &< R(k)+6k^{2}+6k+2 = R(k+1) . \end{aligned} $
>
> So $ L(k+1) < R(k+1) $.
>
> Basis and step together give the result for every $ n\ge3 $. $ \blacksquare $

The step uses only $ k\ge1 $, not $ k\ge3 $: what restricts the theorem to $ n\ge3 $ is the basis, not the step. This is the pattern of inequality proofs by induction: compare the *increments* of the two sides, and show the larger side also grows faster.

## The Picture

The bars show the crossover; the second panel shows the cleaner form the inequality takes.

!(78%)[The cubic overtakes the quadratic at n = 3](problem-10-cubes.svg "A diagram in four parts. The top band gives the step identity for the gap D of n. The left panel compares three n squared plus three n plus one with two n cubed for n from zero to five, the cubic taking the lead at n equal to three. The right panel shows one plus one over n, cubed, against a dashed line at three, crossing below it at n equal to three. Two bands explain why the basis is three and compare the increments of the two sides.")

## Remark — A Hidden Difference of Cubes

The left side is not an arbitrary quadratic. By the binomial theorem,

$$$
(n+1)^{3}-n^{3} = 3n^{2}+3n+1 ,
$$$

so the inequality says $ (n+1)^{3}-n^{3} < 2n^{3} $, that is, $ (n+1)^{3} < 3n^{3} $. Dividing by $ n^{3} $,

$$$
\left(1+\frac{1}{n}\right)^{3} < 3 \iff 1+\frac{1}{n} < \sqrt[3]{3} \iff n > \frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{3}-1} \approx 2.2612 .
$$$

The cubic $ 2x^{3}-3x^{2}-3x-1 $ has exactly this one real root, so the inequality holds for real $ x $ above $ 2.2612 $ and fails below it. The first natural number past the root is $ 3 $, which is why the problem starts there. In the figure's right panel the values $ 8, 3.375, 2.370, 1.953,\ldots $ of the left-hand side fall below $ 3 $ exactly at $ n=3 $.

This gives a proof without induction: for $ n\ge3 $, $ (1+1/n)^{3} \le (4/3)^{3} = 64/27 < 3 $.

## Remark — The Basis Is Forced

The claim fails at each of the earlier candidates:

$$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
n & 3n^{2}+3n+1 & 2n^{3} & \text{holds?} \\ \hline
0 & 1 & 0 & \text{no} \\
1 & 7 & 2 & \text{no} \\
2 & 19 & 16 & \text{no} \\
3 & 37 & 54 & \text{yes}
\end{array}
$$$

Yet the inductive step is valid from $ k=1 $ onward. So a proof starting at $ n=1 $ would have a sound step and a false basis, the situation of 5.3 Problem 9 but now only for a finite stretch. Starting at $ 3 $ is not a matter of taste: it is the first point where the basis is true. In general, induction may start from any natural number $ b $, and it then proves the claim for exactly the $ n\ge b $.

## Remark — The Increments Tell the Story

The two increments are themselves linked. Since $ L(k) = (k+1)^{3}-k^{3} $,

$$$
R(k+1)-R(k) = 2\left((k+1)^{3}-k^{3}\right) = 2L(k) ,
$$$

while $ L(k+1)-L(k) = 6(k+1) $; both identities were checked for $ k $ up to $ 1000 $. The right side grows by twice the current value of the left side, which quickly dwarfs the left side's linear increment. Once the cubic is ahead it pulls away: the ratio $ L(n)/R(n) $ falls from $ 0.685 $ at $ n=3 $ to $ 0.015 $ at $ n=100 $, and tends to $ 0 $.

The same identity drives the power sums of 5.2: summing $ (k+1)^{3}-k^{3} = 3k^{2}+3k+1 $ from $ k=0 $ to $ n-1 $ telescopes to $ n^{3} $, which is how the formula for $ \sum k^{2} $ is usually derived.
